==============================================================================================
 #2  RUNNERS = MULTIPLICATION MAPS: M_v.M_w = M_{vw}; (Z/N)* is a GROUP (the atoms)
==============================================================================================
  (Z/10)* = [1, 3, 7, 9]: |.|=phi=4; closed under M_v.M_w? True; every M_v invertible? True
     element orders {1: 1, 3: 4, 7: 4, 9: 2}; cyclic? True  (=> runners on N-torsion form an abelian group)
  (Z/12)* = [1, 5, 7, 11]: |.|=phi=4; closed under M_v.M_w? True; every M_v invertible? True
     element orders {1: 1, 5: 2, 7: 2, 11: 2}; cyclic? False  (=> runners on N-torsion form an abelian group)
  (Z/14)* = [1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13]: |.|=phi=6; closed under M_v.M_w? True; every M_v invertible? True
     element orders {1: 1, 3: 6, 5: 6, 9: 3, 11: 3, 13: 2}; cyclic? True  (=> runners on N-torsion form an abelian group)
  RUNNER COMPOSITION is literally speed-multiplication: applying runner v then runner w = runner vw.
  Scale-invariance THM-522 = the action of M_c; the census atoms are one (Z/N)*-orbit.

==============================================================================================
 #6  OPUC / BLASCHKE MAPS: b_a(z)=(z-a)/(1-conj(a)z) form a GROUP; iterate -> boundary
==============================================================================================
  b_a1 . b_a2 (a1=(0.3+0.4j), a2=(-0.2+0.5j)) maps disk->disk (unit-disk automorphism)? True
     => Blaschke maps are closed under composition = the group of disk automorphisms SU(1,1)/pm.
  iterate b_a, |a|=0.5: |orbit of 0| = [0.5, 0.8, 0.9286, 0.9756, 0.9918, 0.9973]  (|a|->1 => orbit -> unit circle = 'atomic/tight' limit)
  iterate b_a, |a|=0.9: |orbit of 0| = [0.9, 0.9945, 0.9997, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0]  (|a|->1 => orbit -> unit circle = 'atomic/tight' limit)
  iterate b_a, |a|=0.98: |orbit of 0| = [0.98, 0.9998, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0]  (|a|->1 => orbit -> unit circle = 'atomic/tight' limit)
  The OPUC Szego recursion IS Blaschke composition; the extremizer's |alpha_k|->1 (HYP-3793) is the
  orbit hitting the boundary = the loose measure collapsing onto its (Z/N)* atoms.

==============================================================================================
 (c) THE DEDEKIND COCYCLE connects additive #1 <-> modular #6/#7; value -> -1/12=zeta(-1)
==============================================================================================
  s(1,7)=5/14 ; reciprocity s(h,k)+s(k,h)=5/14 =?= 5/14  True
  s(2,7)=1/14 ; reciprocity s(h,k)+s(k,h)=1/14 =?= 1/14  True
  s(3,7)=-1/14 ; reciprocity s(h,k)+s(k,h)=-1/63 =?= -1/63  True
  s(1,13)=11/13 ; reciprocity s(h,k)+s(k,h)=11/13 =?= 11/13  True
  s(5,12)=-1/72 ; reciprocity s(h,k)+s(k,h)=-1/72 =?= -1/72  True
  s(1,k)=(k-1)(k-2)/(12k): ['5/14', '11/13', '13/14'] -> the 1/12 = -zeta(-1) reciprocity constant.

